  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/7/79/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_6.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The splash page promises that the adventure offered this month will show <i>why</i> Spider-Man has \"become America's super sensation!\"\n\nA man-sized Lizard wearing pants and a lab coat appears for the first time in a swamp in the Florida Everglades and chases off what he calls \"puny humans,\" using his great strength to drive them away from the swamp.&nbsp; News about the super-strong Lizard creature quickly spreads all over the nation -- even as far north as New York City, where the <i>Daily Bugle</i> prints a headline challenging Spider-Man to defeat the Lizard!\n\nNot interested in the challenge so much as a possible trip to Florida to snap photos for the paper, Peter Parker goes to visit J. Jonah Jameson and volunteers to head down and get pictures of the Lizard.&nbsp; But Jameson admits that he only printed the challenge in the paper \"to sell papers!\"&nbsp; And he doesn't even really believe that this Lizard character is even <i>real</i>.&nbsp; Regardless, he's not going to spend money to send Parker down there so he can \"waste time.\"\n\nNevertheless, Peter learns all he can about lizards (from the Natural History Museum), and in the process, has to save Liz from a couple of crooks who tried to steal a ruby from one of the museum's display cases, ultimately using Liz as a hostage.&nbsp; Liz is <i>thrilled</i> at being saved by Spider-Man, but it isn't getting Peter any closer to Florida.\n\nFinally, Spider-Man himself shows up at Jameson's office and <i>agrees</i> to accept the challenge, while at the same time suggesting the <i>Bugle</i> send a photographer down to Florida to cover the story.&nbsp; Sure enough, the moment Spider-Man leaves, Jameson contacts Peter Parker and surprises him by announcing that he's going along as <i>well</i>!&nbsp; It's too big of a story to trust to the young photographer.\n\nAfter getting permission from Aunt May (who only agrees to it because he's going to be accompanied by that \"nice man you do part-time work for\"), Peter meets Jameson at the airport.&nbsp; On the plane ride down, Peter educates himself with reading material about lizards, clippings about the locale this new Lizard has been spotted in, and even reads about a Dr. Curtis Connors, a reptile expert who lives near there (who he hopes can be of assistance).\n\nOnce in Florida, Peter excuses himself to buy some film supplies, then changes to Spider-Man and heads directly for the swamp where the Lizard has been seen.&nbsp; But his approach is detected by the Lizard, ambushed, and the two soon come to blows.&nbsp; During the brief battle, Spider-Man notes that he's not wearing any kind of disguise -- \"that grotesque form is really <i>him</i>!\"\n\nSpider-Man completely underestimates his strength and suddenly finds himself hurled bodily about a half mile away, where it takes him to the vicinity of Dr. Connors' home, and so he decides to pay a call there and warn them that the Lizard is nearby.&nbsp; But when he approaches and sees a woman weeping inside, he learns it is the wife of Dr. Connors.&nbsp; And in the next moment, Spider-Man is told that her husband <i>is </i>the Lizard!\n\nShe then explains how her husband sought a way to regrow his right arm that he lost during the war, being particularly fascinated by reptiles and their ability to regrow limbs.&nbsp; He soon \"became one of the world's leading authorities on reptiles.\"&nbsp; He became obsessed at the possibility of being able to introduce such an ability to the human race.\n\nConnors worked tirelessly on finding a serum, while at the same time being a good father to their son, Billy.&nbsp; After an experiment on a test rabbit produced a regrown leg, there was nothing left to do but test his formula on himself -- the first <i>human</i> subject.\n\nAnd before his wife could stop him, he drained the vial of serum.&nbsp; Moments later, he stared with delight down at his newly-regrown right hand!\n\nBut... the growth didn't end there, and his body began to mutate slowly into a human-sized <b>lizard</b>!&nbsp; Unwilling for his family to see him like this, the reptilian creature fled into the night.&nbsp; He returned the next day to try and find a cure for himself, but sensing his brain gradually affected (and dulled by the transformation), he used the last of his humanity to scribble down a note of warning to his wife.\n\n\"Take Billy... leave -- never come back -\"\n\nMartha Connors' story is interrupted by a cry for help from outside.&nbsp; <i>It's Billy</i>, who she thought was taking a nap in another room.&nbsp; Instead, he's out wandering around the swamp, finally being scared away by the sight of the Lizard and calling out.&nbsp; Spider-Man immediately races out to try and find him and encounters the Lizard for the second time, noting how <i>hard</i> his armored hide is.&nbsp; (But the Lizard seems strangely attracted to the boy.)\n\nThe sound of Martha's voice calling out for Billy seems to rattle the Lizard, and he abruptly retreats.&nbsp; Billy then recognizes Spider-Man from pictures he's seen, and then the science prodigy in disguise asks the Connors to see the doctor's notes.&nbsp; Perhaps he can find some kind of antidote, with their help,\n\nSpider-Man works for hours on a cure to the Lizard's condition, finally believing that he's found it.&nbsp; But as he holds up the serum, he wonders \"How am I going to get him to <i>drink</i> it??\"\n\nBut as it turns out, Spider-Man at least won't have to hunt down the Lizard, because at the moment, he barges into the house, angry that this Spider-Man character doesn't fear him like all the other humans do.&nbsp; And it's clear now that he no longer recognizes his own wife and son, becoming less human by the minute.\n\nHe uses his superior strength to attack Spider-Man, initially believing he's eliminated him, then moves into the house, stating out loud his intention to \"feed my serum to <i>other</i> lizards, crocodiles, alligators!!&nbsp; I'll build a mighty lizard army!\"\n\nBut after he storms into the lab (presumably to grab the serum) and heads outside for the swamp again, Spider-Man finally recovers, much to the relief of Martha Connors.&nbsp; Both of them have heard the Lizard's new plan and realize that there are so many reptiles on Earth, that if the Lizard's plan succeeds, \"mankind wouldn't stand a <i>chance</i>!\"\n\nAs there's no time to call for help, Spider-Man heads out to try and stop the Lizard before he can infect the various reptiles, realizing that, despite the threat the Lizard poses, there's still a man trapped inside -- \"a decent, talented man!\"&nbsp; He <i>has </i>to find a way to defeat him without harming the Lizard himself, but <i>before</i> he can successfully mutate ordinary lizards into a huge army to attack humans with.\n\nThankfully, the Lizard's trail is still fresh, and Spider-Man soon catches up to him near an abandoned Spanish fort near a body of water.&nbsp; He spots the Lizard talking to a group of alligators.&nbsp; Pausing to snap a few photos, Spider-Man overhears him telling the alligators that as soon as he dumps his serum into the swamp waters, the millions of reptiles in the Everglades <i>alone</i> will see \"the birth of a new race of lizard creatures\" with him positioned as their master.\n\nBut he still needs time to make some final adjustments to the serum and heads toward the fort.&nbsp; To his horror, Spider-Man's perch on one of its walls crumbles away, and he finds himself tumbling down to the ground near the Lizard and his alligator allies.&nbsp; It seems that the moment of attack has been chosen <i>for</i> him.\n\nWithout hesitation, the Lizard orders the alligators to attack, driving Spider-Man up another wall.&nbsp; But he soon finds the Lizard capable of scaling the rock walls <i>too</i>, and he reaches the top first, trapping the hero between the alligators below and the Lizard's deadly tail, forcing the hero to swing away.\n\nBut the Lizard easily follows and corners him inside the walls of the fort.&nbsp; Spider-Man at least bolts closed a door, ensuring that the nearby alligators are locked outside.&nbsp; It's just the two of them now!&nbsp; Both of them scale the walls to the top, Spider-Man desperately trying to stay out of the Lizard's reach.\n\nFinally, Spider-Man knocks the Lizard off the wall, and as they fall, he grabs the serum from his belt and dumps it down the surprised Lizard's throat.&nbsp; But to his horror, it doesn't seem to have taken effect, and the Lizard's tail swats out at him once they reach the ground again.\n\nBut just as the Lizard moves in for the kill, he suddenly grabs at his head -- <i>what's happening</i>?\n\nThe serum has worked, and in the next moment, the Lizard's scaly hide reverts to human flesh, and soon the creature returns to human form... and becomes Dr. Curtis Connors once again (and his new arm shrivels away once more).\n\nWithout the instructions of the Lizard directing them, the alligators outside soon wander off, and Spider-Man heads back to the Connors' home with the doctor in tow.&nbsp; Martha greets him warmly, happy to have her husband back.\n\nDr. Connors then burns his notes so that his experiment will never be repeated, and since no laws were actually broken, Spider-Man agrees to keep the secret that the Lizard was Dr. Connors.&nbsp; The happy couple thank Spider-Man for his help before he departs.\n\nBy the time Peter returns to the Florida hotel to present his photos to Jameson, he finds the publisher convinced that the entire Lizard persona was \"some sort of <i>publicity stunt</i>!\"&nbsp; And he rips up Peter's photos, asserting that the so-called Lizard was never real and that the photos Peter hands him are fakes.\n\nBack home, Peter Parker's call to Liz Allan for a date reveals that she has eyes only for Spider-Man (who rescued her), meaning Peter has become his <i>own competition</i>!\n\nAnd as for Spider-Man, he immediately mails a letter to the <i>Bugle</i> offices, which Betty opens and reads to Jameson.&nbsp; It says, \"Roses are red, Violets are blue... I'm still at large, so phooey to you!\"&nbsp; Jameson swears to get him if it's the last thing he does!",
  "grade_one_liner": "Connors' tragic fall lands hard, while Billy's swamp detour undercuts the moment.\nA solid, engaging chapter where the people shine more than the Lizard ever does.",
  "subtitle": "A Touch of Heartbreak -- A Dash of Humor",
  "review_text": "A brand new foe for Spider-Man leads him down to the Florida Everglades for his first (and seemingly <i>only</i>) encounter with the Lizard.\n\nOn the surface, a Lizard-type foe whose master plan is to infect the world's reptiles and take over the world with his mutated army doesn't seem likely to inspire the imaginations of readers, and we can be grateful to the title hero for sparing us all from such a horrible fate.\n\nBut despite the relative blandness of the villain (and <b>be honest</b>:&nbsp; can anyone claim that [SPAN: severity=warning; text=the Lizard is on the same playing field as  Doctor Octopus or Sandman?]), the issue did enough to keep the reader invested through the first half of the book.\n\nWhat was strikingly [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=fresh] in this issue was the playful banter of Spider-Man while engaging his enemies.&nbsp; That seemed to be on display here more than ever before, starting with the short but lively aside at the Natural History Museum, where Spider-Man takes down a couple of crooks (which coincidentally also put Peter Parker in the unusual position of becoming his own competition with the lovely Liz Allan).\n\nAnd then he immediately follows that up by having a one-on-one chat with J. Jonah Jameson:\n[ASSET: center | SpideyAndJameson | width=67%]\n\nSuffice it to say that I found the interplay between Spider-Man and Jameson [SPAN: severity=success; text=quite comical], even though the goal of the meeting was simply to convince Jameson to fund a trip to Florida.&nbsp; (Credit still to Stan and Steve for making that \"meeting\" memorable!)\n\n[ASSET: left | NiceManJameson | width=11%] Another nice aside was the moment when Peter had to get permission from his aunt to travel to Florida, receiving her approval only after he indicated that the \"nice man\" he worked for at the Bugle would be going <i>with</i> him.\n[BR]\n[BR] Peter assures his aunt that Jameson is [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"like a big, fat guardian angel, Aunt May!\"]\n[BR] (eliciting a loud guffaw from me, <i>heh</i>).\n\nSuch homey moments <i>do</i> contribute to the assertion on the splash page about how Spider-Man has grown ever more popular with readers.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | FirstEncounter | width=8%] But by page 7, with Peter and Jameson finally down in Florida, it was time for the hero to meet the villain for the first time, and there he quips that he's \"doomed\" once more.\n[BR]\n[BR] The brief battle between the two conveniently leaves him outside the house of the very doctor he's chosen to look up once he arrives in Florida.\n\n<i>Sure</i>, Spider-Man's arrival in Florida followed by an <i>immediate download</i> of Connors' backstory -- [SPAN: severity=warning; text=it would be easy to classify this as a stretch], but these sorts of coincidences occur in comics all the time.\n\nNonetheless, the actual information provided to Spider-Man -- in the form of a backstory that Martha Connors relays to him -- [SPAN: severity=info; text=<i>that</i> was genuinely riveting, especially in how clearly it laid out Connors' desperation and hope.]&nbsp; It was easy enough to imagine how a one-armed scientist would be motivated enough to seek a way to regrow his own lost arm and introduce such healing ability to humans.\n\nI remained transfixed as Stan Lee and Steve Ditko guided me along the events, [SPAN: severity=success; text=and throughout the entirety of the first 10 pages, I really had no reason to complain.]\n[ASSET: center | TestingItOnHimself | width=67%]\n\nAnd let's be honest -- I can't imagine <i>any</i> reader claiming that they'll <i>ever</i> forget the thrill on the face of Dr. Connors when he stares excitedly down at his newly regrown right arm -- a moment made even more tragic in hindsight.&nbsp; To use his own words, it was, indeed, [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=\"the greatest medical feat of all time!\"]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=But of course, it all goes horribly wrong from there...]\n\nThe sad backstory of the Lizard was <b>heartbreaking</b>, especially the panel on page 11 that shows the Lizard scribbling down a warning to his family to flee the area as he feels the last bit of his humanity fading away.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=That's a sobering moment.]\n\nAnd while Martha's reluctance to abandon her husband -- or their home -- is <i>understandable</i>, the rest of page 11 that shows young Billy blundering around the swamp is [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=far less believable] (and even borderline <b>dumb</b> -- [SPAN: severity=warning; text=what mother wouldn't know <i>exactly</i> where her young son was during his nap?])\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | IWillDestroyYou | width=15%] Luckily, Spider-Man is there to rescue the young boy, keep the family safe when the Lizard returns to the house to retrieve his serum, and even uses his scientific knowledge to develop a cure ([SPAN: severity=info; text=which is <i>no small achievement</i> for a high school student, I might add]).\n[BR]\n[BR] Spider-Man heads off to administer the cure, leading to their final conflict inside the crumbling walls of an old Spanish fort.\n\nThankfully, he is able to splash the liquid down the Lizard's throat:\n[ASSET: center | BecomingHumanAgain | width=67%]\n\nThe monster is cured and the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved.]\n\nSpider-Man's subsequent kind treatment of Dr. Connors and his family could even be classified as <i>tender</i>.&nbsp; There was a warmth in their encounter that cannot be denied, and seeing Connors' gratitude was genuinely satisfying.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nFinally back home, we get one more chance to see Spider-Man having fun at Jameson's expense.&nbsp; It seems he's finally had enough of the publisher, who endeavors to drag Spider-Man's name through the mud every chance he gets.\n\n[ASSET: left | PhooeyToYou | width=14%] Spider-Man seems to have no problem webbing Jameson's mouth shut -- much to the publisher's sputtering outrage, gluing him to his chair, dangling him from ceilings, or even mailing him nasty-grams, <i>heh</i>.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=And who can <i>blame</i> him?]&nbsp; It's one of the book's reliable charms and adds a little humor to a story featuring a largely uninspired (and apparently one-off) villain.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe splash page's promise that this adventure will show why Spider-Man has become so popular -- [SPAN: severity=error; text=I found it largely hyperbolic.]&nbsp; As far as I'm concerned, the story moved along well enough, particularly in its first half, and it <i>did</i> admittedly contain some of the personal moments that make Spider-Man and Peter Parker unique...\n\nBut to claim that it actually showed why Spider-Man has [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"become America's super sensation,\"] -- that's a bit of a stretch.&nbsp; The Announcements section of the letters page included a \"personal message\" from Stan Lee and Steve Ditko that thanked the many readers for their interest in and support of this magazine.&nbsp; Perhaps they were referring to this...&nbsp; I don't know.\n\nThe notion of a Lizard with a master plan to rule the world alongside his reptilian army -- [SPAN: severity=warning; text=that was perhaps too far-fetched for my tastes.]&nbsp; Yet there's no denying the humanity aspect of the tale, both in the Peter Parker moments in NYC and in the tender interactions he has with the Connors family in Florida.\n\nAnd let's not forget the quips and comedy moments with Jameson -- [SPAN: severity=success; text=<i>definitely</i> a strength.]\n\nA story like this may not adequately demonstrate why Spider-Man is \"America's super sensation,\" and the Lizard may not stack up as a major player alongside Spider-Man's ever-growing rogues' gallery, but Stan and Steve do enough here to land the story solidly in mid‑grade territory and show that even when an issue isn't top-notch, it still proves to be a worthwhile read.\n\nAs for next month, the final panel promises the [SPAN: severity=warning; text=sensational return of the <b>Vulture</b>!]",
  "highlight": "Spider‑Man takes on the Lizard, but it's Connors' humanity that does the real heavy lifting."
}